# Ronda — copy to .env and edit.  cp .env.example .env
#
# This file ships pointing at the Docker Compose Postgres, so a clean clone
# runs with no accounts to create and no internet beyond `docker pull`.

# ── Cameras ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CAMERAS is the ordered list of camera slugs. A slug is three things at once —
# a MediaMTX path name, a directory under recordings/, and the middle of the
# variable names below — so it is lowercase letters and digits only, starting
# with a letter. No underscore (MediaMTX splits path names on `_`), no dash and
# no capitals (neither survives an environment variable name).
#
# Choose them once. Renaming a slug later means moving its recordings directory
# and rewriting stream_events and daily_coverage by hand: both foreign keys are
# ON DELETE CASCADE, not ON UPDATE. The human-readable label lives in
# CAMERA_<SLUG>_NAME and IS cheap to change.
CAMERAS=yard,cam2,cam3,cam4,cam5,cam6,cam7

# Per camera, three variables:
#
#   CAMERA_<SLUG>_NAME        the operator's label. Optional; defaults to the
#                             capitalised slug. Never used as an identifier.
#   CAMERA_<SLUG>_RTSP_MAIN   recorded continuously, never watched directly.
#   CAMERA_<SLUG>_RTSP_SUB    live view only, so watching can never disturb the
#                             recording (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#the-media-pipeline).
#
# The URLs are written out in full rather than assembled from an IP and a
# password, because there is no single URL shape that holds: one camera in this
# family serves
#     rtsp://<ip>:5543/<md5(ONVIF_PASSWORD)>/live/channel0
# and another serves
#     rtsp://<user>:<pass>@<ip>:554/V_ENC_000
# on the standard port with credentials in userinfo. Guessing wrong renders a
# config that starts cleanly and never connects, which is the worst way for
# this to fail.
#
# Ask the camera instead. Every ONVIF camera will tell you its own stream URLs:
# enable ONVIF in the vendor app, then run a WS-Discovery probe to find the
# device service (it is often on a non-obvious port) and call GetStreamUri on
# each profile. `onvif-cli`, `python-onvif-zeep` or ONVIF Device Manager all do
# this; the URLs they return are what belongs below.
#
# A stream URL is a CREDENTIAL either way — a password in userinfo or a hash in
# the path. mediamtx.yml embeds them all and is gitignored for that reason, and
# doctor, seed and render:mediamtx mask them when printing.
#
# `publisher` instead of a URL declares a camera with no hardware yet: the
# matching fakecam service in docker-compose.yml publishes the test fixture to
# it, and the _SUB below points at MediaMTX's own copy of that path, which it
# relays on demand — no second ffmpeg, no transcode. Swap in the real URLs when
# the camera is on the wall.
#
# After changing any of these, re-render AND restart MediaMTX — it reads the
# rendered file at startup, so a render alone changes nothing:
#     cd apps/api && bun run render:mediamtx && cd ../.. && docker compose restart mediamtx

# ── What to set on the camera itself ────────────────────────────────────────
# None of this is read by the app - it is configured in the camera's own web UI
# - but it decides everything about storage, and it is the first thing anyone
# setting this up gets wrong. For human review of a small store:
#
#   Resolution      1920x1080 (main stream)
#   Framerate       15 fps
#   Codec           H.264, High profile
#   Rate control    VBR with a MAX BITRATE of 2 Mbps - capped, not unlimited
#   I-frame interval 30 (2 seconds at 15 fps)
#   Min shutter     ~1/100s if the shop is dim, so hands do not smear
#   Sub-stream      H.264, low resolution - `doctor` FAILS if it is not H.264,
#                   because live view would otherwise need transcoding this
#                   project deliberately does not do
#
# Why 15 fps and not 30: bitrate is spread across frames, so halving the
# framerate doubles the bits each frame gets. 15 fps at 1.8 Mbps gives ~120 kbit
# per frame against ~83 kbit at 30 fps and 2.5 Mbps - smaller AND sharper. Human
# review needs per-frame clarity (a face, what is in a hand), not smooth motion.
# Revisit this if the footage is ever training data for computer vision, where
# the trade inverts and you want the frames back.
#
# Why a CAPPED VBR rather than plain VBR: unbounded VBR makes the storage
# projection a guess, and the failure mode here is a full disk stopping all
# seven cameras at once. A ceiling turns the projection into an upper bound.

CAMERA_YARD_NAME=Yard
CAMERA_YARD_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_YARD_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/yard

CAMERA_CAM2_NAME=Camera 2
CAMERA_CAM2_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_CAM2_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/cam2

CAMERA_CAM3_NAME=Camera 3
CAMERA_CAM3_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_CAM3_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/cam3

CAMERA_CAM4_NAME=Camera 4
CAMERA_CAM4_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_CAM4_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/cam4

CAMERA_CAM5_NAME=Camera 5
CAMERA_CAM5_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_CAM5_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/cam5

CAMERA_CAM6_NAME=Camera 6
CAMERA_CAM6_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_CAM6_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/cam6

CAMERA_CAM7_NAME=Camera 7
CAMERA_CAM7_RTSP_MAIN=publisher
CAMERA_CAM7_RTSP_SUB=rtsp://localhost:8554/cam7

# Which stream live view watches. Default (unset, or anything other than
# `main`) is the sub-stream: low bitrate, H.264, a separate pull from the
# camera, so watching can never disturb the recording and the picture plays in
# any browser.
#
# `main` watches the recorded path at full resolution instead. Two costs, both
# real:
#   - the main stream may be H.265, which needs a hardware decoder. It plays in
#     Chrome and Safari on a machine that has one and shows a blank frame on a
#     machine that does not. The sub-stream always plays.
#   - it gives up the guarantee that no request can reach the recorded path.
# It does NOT cost the recording: the main path is sourceOnDemand: no, so it is
# already pulled around the clock and a viewer attaches to that stream rather
# than opening a second one.
# LIVE_SOURCE=sub

# ── MediaMTX ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All loopback: the authenticated Hono API is the only way to reach a stream.
MEDIAMTX_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9997
MEDIAMTX_WEBRTC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8889
MEDIAMTX_PLAYBACK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9996

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two URLs, one job each. DATABASE_URL is pooled (PgBouncer, `-pooler` host on
# Neon) and used at runtime. DATABASE_URL_DIRECT is unpooled and used ONLY by
# drizzle-kit: PgBouncer in transaction mode rejects the session-level
# statements migrations issue.
#
# Port 5439, not 5432: the standard port is usually taken by a native
# Postgres install. See the note in docker-compose.yml.
#
# To use Neon instead, swap in the pooled and unpooled connection strings and
# re-run the migration. Keep sslmode=require&channel_binding=require.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://ronda:ronda@127.0.0.1:5439/ronda
DATABASE_URL_DIRECT=postgres://ronda:ronda@127.0.0.1:5439/ronda

# ── Recording ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RECORDINGS_DIR=./recordings

# How long MediaMTX keeps footage, per camera. Deletion is by AGE only: there is
# no global size cap and nothing evicts under disk pressure, so if this times the
# number of cameras exceeds the disk, recording stops on every camera at once
# rather than degrading. /health projects daysRemaining from measured bytes and
# will count down to it, but nothing acts on that warning.
#
# THE SHIPPED VALUE IS FOR DEVELOPMENT, not for a real install. A clean clone
# runs seven fake cameras against a laptop disk, and they write ~31 GB/day
# between them.
#
# For the reference install - seven 1080p15 cameras at 2 Mbps on a 4 TB drive,
# ~136 GB/day - start at 336h (14 days), run a week, read the real bytesPerHour
# off /health, then raise toward 504h. See the storage table in README.md; the
# numbers there are estimates and that measurement is the fact.
RECORD_DELETE_AFTER=12h

# Per camera, if one needs a different window:
#     CAMERA_<SLUG>_RECORD_DELETE_AFTER=336h
# This exists because retention is a pathDefaults value, so a short fleet-wide
# setting applies to a camera that already has an archive on disk and reaps it
# within minutes of the restart. Run the fake cameras at 12h on a laptop and
# give a real one its full window:
#     RECORD_DELETE_AFTER=12h
#     CAMERA_YARD_RECORD_DELETE_AFTER=336h
# It is also how the till camera keeps three weeks while the stockroom keeps one.
# Integer plus s, m or h. There is no `d`.

# ── Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-up is disabled; the single operator account comes from `bun run db:seed`.
# Generate a secret with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=change-me-generate-a-32-byte-secret
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3001

# The operator account `bun run db:seed` creates. Defaults are baked into the
# script, so a clean clone seeds without touching this file; the README repeats
# them. Change them here for anything that is not a dummy install.
SEED_OPERATOR_EMAIL=operator@ronda.local
SEED_OPERATOR_PASSWORD=ronda-operator

# Origins allowed to call this API with credentials, comma-separated — used for
# both CORS and Better Auth's trustedOrigins. 3000 is `pnpm dev`, 3100 is
# Playwright (apps/web/playwright.config.ts); anything else is refused with 403.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3100

# ── Web → API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The web app never holds a database credential. It talks to Hono; Hono talks
# to Postgres.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3001

# The camera's wall-clock zone, used ONLY at the render boundary: the API speaks
# epoch-ms internally and answers in RFC3339 UTC, and the timeline component is
# what turns that into "27 Aug, 14:20". Mirrors TZ below, which is the same zone
# for the server side.
#
# Next reads apps/web/.env*, NOT this file, so this line is documentation and
# the literal default in components/timeline.tsx is what actually applies. To
# override, put it in apps/web/.env.local.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CAMERA_TZ=Asia/Jakarta

# ── Timeline ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# recordPath writes filenames in the SERVER's local time. Internally everything
# is epoch-ms UTC; this is the camera-local zone used at the render boundary.
TZ=Asia/Jakarta
